<Layout title="About">
  <h1>About</h1>
  <p>
    Hi, I'm Morgan! I'm finishing my PhD in the joint UC
    Berkeley/UCSF Bioengineering program, where I got to spend my days
    doing something I genuinely love: working on incredible science while
    connecting with my community. As a researcher in Dr. Kevin Healy's
    lab, I engineered hyaluronic acid hydrogels that help the body
    regenerate tissue after complex soft tissue injuries like rotator
    cuff injuries. My interdisciplinary background in hydrogel
    engineering, biochemistry, and cell-material interactions lent itself
    to the complexity of this engineering challenge&mdash;designing a
    therapeutic that is effective, safe, and can be used by <em>anyone</em>
    regardless of biology or geography. As a co-inventor of the technology behind MuscleMatrix, and now a
    scientific consultant helping build the company, I get to see that work
    move from the bench toward something that could reach the clinic and
    help patients directly.
  </p>

  <p>
    My path into science started in 10th-grade AP Biology, watching my
    teacher draw a DNA double helix on the whiteboard in thick red marker.
    The way the bases fit together, and the fact that they spelled out the
    code for life, fascinated me (and still does). But a bigger question
    stuck with me: why are there still so many unanswered questions? That
    curiosity is still what drives me: using materials engineering and
    computation to answer complex biological questions, and turning what
    we learn about cells into therapies that help people heal.
  </p>

  <p>
    Before Berkeley, I got to explore a huge range of science, and each
    stop shaped how I approach research today. In the Directed Assembly of
    Particles and Suspensions lab, I fabricated dual-network hydrogels with
    magnetically responsive fiber reinforcement for bone grafts, ran COMSOL
    simulations to chase new toughening mechanisms, and built freeze-cast
    nerve repair conduits alongside collaborators at Dartmouth College and
    Harvard Medical School&mdash;work that taught me just how much
    materials science and mechanics matter for a device to actually survive
    inside the body. I also screened CRISPR guide RNAs at Editas
    Medicine, chased druggable targets for cancer and Sickle Cell Disease
    at UConn Health and Vertex Pharmaceuticals, and worked hands-on with
    patients as an EMT and Clinical Care Technician. That last piece
    matters most to me: it's the reason I never think about a hydrogel as
    purely a materials problem. Somewhere at the other end is a patient,
    and that's who I'm actually designing for.
  </p>

  <p>
    That same belief carries into how I spend my time outside the lab. I
    volunteer weekly with Food Not Bombs East Bay, and I care deeply about
    mutual aid and the social and political forces that determine who
    actually gets access to science, medicine, and care. I'm also
    passionate about <a href="/teaching/">teaching</a>, and about helping other people find and
    chase their own passions in STEM&mdash;whether that's a fellow
    researcher or someone who's never thought of themselves as "a science
    person" before. If that's you, I'd love to talk.
  </p>

  <h2>Milestones</h2>
  <p>
    UC Berkeley/UCSF PhD in Bioengineering (expected October 2026) &middot;
    Northeastern University BS in Biochemistry (2021) &middot; NSF Graduate
    Research Fellow
  </p>
